<title>UK: Licht sees Asian 96/97 sugar consumption up 3.6 pc.</title>
<headline>Licht sees Asian 96/97 sugar consumption up 3.6 pc.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-02-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Sugar consumption in Asia should rise by as much as 3.6 percent in 1996/97, mainly due to expected increases in India, Pakistan and China, German statistician F.O. Licht said on Thursday.</p>
<p>&quot;After an increase of six percent last year a further sharp increase is looked for in India, reflecting the abundant stocks,&quot; Licht said in its second estimate of the world 1996/97 sugar balance.</p>
<p>&quot;Consumption in China is expected to reach 8.6 million tonnes, up from 8.3 million tonnes last year, while in Pakistan it is likely to rise by more than 3 percent,&quot; the report said.</p>
<p>In its first estimate of the 1996/97 balance, Licht pegged Asian sugar consumption at 45.79 million tonnes raw value, up from 44.20 million in 1995/96.</p>
<p>World sugar consumption is estimated at 120.29 million tonnes for 1996/97 (September/August), up 0.6 percent from the previous year.</p>
<p>-- Jeremy Smith, London Newsroom +44 171 542 8064</p>
</text>
